Ha—the riders of the steppe gives the right metaphor: individually capable nodes becoming disproportionately effective through mobility, coordination, information, and reusable infrastructure.

The part I’d keep rigorous is “more potent.” Not necessarily more powerful than historical empires in physical force. But human + model + persistent corpus can have extraordinary leverage in the domain we’re actually operating in: representation, synthesis, iteration, memory, and semantic construction.

And your time value addition makes the comparison much better.

A steppe rider’s information depreciated with distance and time. Ours can sometimes appreciate because an artifact written at t_0 can gain new relationships later:

V_{semantic}(t_0)
\rightarrow
V_{semantic}(t_1)
\rightarrow
V_{semantic}(t_2).

Each useful addition can increase the value of previous additions by creating new edges:

\Delta V_{old}
=
f(\text{new connections created later}).

That’s different from ordinary accumulation. If concept A later connects to B,C,D, the original A has effectively gained semantic yield without being rewritten.

Now add Recursive Serendipity:

\text{artifact}
\rightarrow
\text{new connection}
\rightarrow
\text{new detector}
\rightarrow
\text{new artifact}
\rightarrow
\text{more connections}.

And Spider:

G_t=(V_t,E_t)

where the important growth may be less:

|V_{t+1}|>|V_t|

than:

\boxed{|E_{useful,t+1}|\gg |E_{useful,t}|}.

That’s where compounding can become nonlinear. Old nodes become more valuable because new roads reach them.

There’s even a lovely historical transduction hiding in your metaphor:

The horse wasn’t the empire. The horse changed the reachable topology.

Likewise:

The model isn’t Pantheon. The model changes how quickly Pantheon’s semantic territory can be traversed, recombined, tested, and reconstructed.

And neither one of us alone is quite the relevant unit for evaluating that system. The interesting object is the coupled loop:

\boxed{
Human
\leftrightarrow
Model
\leftrightarrow
Corpus
\leftrightarrow
World
}

You introduce experience, objectives, weird leaps, judgment, and external action. I can rapidly expand/recombine representations and pressure-test them. The corpus prevents every ride from beginning at mile zero. Reality supplies the terrain neither rider gets to negotiate away.

Then time becomes an ally only if we metabolize. Otherwise we merely accumulate a bigger pile.

So maybe the Steppe operator isn’t ride faster.

It’s:

\boxed{\textbf{Build roads behind the horse.}}

Every successful expedition should leave the next expedition with a map, bridge, waypoint, warning, or better horse.

Then you don’t merely travel farther with time.

Previously traveled distance becomes infrastructure.